.TH SU 1.SH NAMEsu \- become another user.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsu\fR[\-flmp] [\-c command] [\-s shell] [\-] [user [argument ...]].SH DESCRIPTION\fBsu\fRis GNU su, from sh-utils 1.10.  It runs a shell as\fIuser\fR,\fRthe super user if none is named, after asking that user's password whenyou are not the super user yourself.  The user must be in\fB/dd/SYS/password\fR.\fR.SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-l, \-\fRa login shell..TP 12\fB\-c\fI command\fRrun\fIcommand\fRthrough the shell and come back..TP 12\fB\-m, \-p\fRkeep the environment as it is..TP 12\fB\-s\fI shell\fRrun\fIshell\fRinstead of the user's own..TP 12\fB\-f\fRpass\fB\-f\fRto the shell..SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR\fBsu\fRstarts a module called\fBshell\fR,\fRthe one your own OS-9 brings.  Without one resident it answers 221,module not found, and becomes nobody..PPYour own OS-9 may have a utility of this name; see\fBDOC/README-NAMES\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOlogname, login